<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bases Orgánicas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Organic Bases of the Mexican Republic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bases Orgánicas were a centralist constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, drafted by a junta of notables under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> and promulgated on 12 June 1843. They replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a> of 1836, kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> under central control and strengthened the presidency. They remained in force until the federal constitution of 1824 was restored in 1846.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bases Orgánicas arose from the nineteenth-century struggle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> between conservative centralists and liberal federalists. The federalist constitution of 1824 had given way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a>, which ended the First Mexican Republic and began the Centralist Republic of Mexico. Instability continued under the new system: Yucatan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> seceded, and there were many rebellions in favour of restoring federalism.</p><p>On 8 August 1841 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-paredes">Mariano Paredes</a> rose against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> and was joined at Tacubaya by the forces of Santa Anna and Gabriel Valencia. Bustamante surrendered power through the Estanzuela Accords on 6 October 1841, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> issued the Bases de Tacubaya, which called for a constituent congress to write a new constitution.</p><p>The congress installed on 10 June 1842 proved strongly federalist. On 11 December a pronunciamiento at Huejotzingo, instigated by Santa Anna through the minister of war José María Tornel, demanded its dissolution, and on 19 December President Nicolás Bravo approved a decree replacing it with a Council of Notables of eighty men, which produced the new constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The constitution kept the division of the country, first made in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a>, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> rather than federal states. Departmental governors were appointed by the president from nominees of the departmental assemblies, which were popularly elected but largely confined to policing. Only a male citizen with an annual income of two hundred pesos could vote, which excluded most labourers, and elections passed through primary juntas and primary and secondary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> to a departmental electoral college.</p><p>The president was elected by the departmental assemblies for five years. He held substantial veto powers over congress, could pass local legislation for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> through exceptional powers, and appointed a presidential council of seventeen members and four ministers, of justice, finance, war and relations.</p><p>Congress had two chambers. The chamber of deputies had one deputy for every seventy thousand inhabitants. Two thirds of the 63 senators were elected by the departmental assemblies from among landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, mine owners, merchants and manufacturers, the rest by the deputies, the president and the supreme court. Judicial power lay with a Supreme Court, local courts and a perpetual court martial appointed by the president.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bases Orgánicas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Presidents changed often, among them Anastasio Bustamante, Santa Anna and Mariano Paredes, and the <b>Bases Orgánicas</b> of 1843 replaced the Siete Leyes.</blockquote><a href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
